{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789291023,"nonce":"fJGy1IB8dlTiPDFtQKcx2970utE=","message":"Redesigned 2026-09-13 after the owner rejected generated Wren source\n(\"Caribou_.call_(this, \\\"hit\\\", a0)\") as an FFI in Wren syntax. The owner\nowns both compilers, so the binding happens where each compiler already\nbinds names against types, not in generated source at run time.\n\nThe principle: a class from the other language is installed into this\ncompiler's own class table as one of its own classes, whose methods\ntarget the bridge. After that, every pass, tier and optimiser treats it\nas native: sema knows its type, the JIT can inline-cache or devirtualise\nits calls, the same as a class written in this language.\n\nWren side (wren_lift): a Haxe class published in the registry is\ninstalled as a ClassMir: name, superclass, num_fields = 0 (fields live in\nthe Haxe object), and one MethodMir per Haxe method/getter/setter/ctor\nwhose body is a stub MIR function of a new instruction, `CallBridge {\ntarget: BridgeTarget, args }`, that the tiers lower to a direct call into\nthe adapter with the bridge Callable baked in (typed: the Haxe function's\nsignature; the receiver is the proxy's Haxe object). sema types its\nreturns from the interface (Int/Float/Bool -\u003e Num, String -\u003e String,\nObject -\u003e that class), so downstream inference is as precise as for Wren\ncode. The import `import \"game:Player\" for Player` resolves through the\nexisting resolve_module_fn to a module the adapter installs directly\n(install_module_mir, which exists for .wlbc), not to source. Objects: the\nproxy is an ObjInstance of that installed class with one hidden slot\nholding the HaxeRef handle; construction calls the Haxe ctor and fills\nthe slot. This needs a wren_lift change (the CallBridge instruction and a\nhook to install an externally built ClassMir): file it on wren_lift's\ntracker as the seam's compile-time half.\n\nHaxe side (ash): a Wren class published in the registry is installed\ninto the decoded bytecode's type table as an HOBJ HLTypeObj with fields\n(none; the object is a proxy) and protos whose findexes point at new\nHLNative entries of library \"caribou\" (the natives table is Vec and\nextendable at load), each native resolved by ash's resolver to an adapter\nfunction that performs the bridge call with the Wren method baked in.\nThe Haxe compiler still needs the extern at compile time: the build\nmacro emits `extern class Hud { function new(p:Player); function\ndraw():Void; }` under the configured namespace's package from the\nregistry interface (Wren declaration file where present, Dynamic\notherwise), with @:hlNative(\"caribou\", \"hud.Hud.draw\") on each method so\nthe Haxe compiler itself emits the native call. That is the standard\nHashLink extern mechanism; ash needs no change for it beyond accepting\nnatives it resolves through the seam. Objects: the proxy is an instance\nof that HOBJ carrying the Wren object's handle in one hidden field.\n\nBoth directions therefore compile to ordinary calls in each language's\nown IR, and the bridge is what the call lands in. DCE: the build macro\nalso applies @:keep to published Haxe classes (previous comment);\nwren_lift has no DCE at that level.\n\nOrder of work: (1) wren_lift seam: CallBridge + install_external_class\nhook, on wren_lift's tracker; (2) caribou-wren installs published Haxe\nclasses as ClassMir and the interop test becomes `import \"game:Player\"`\nwith no generated source; (3) ash side: registry-driven HLNative entries\nresolved through the seam; (4) the build macro. The generated-source\nwork in crates/caribou-wren/src/import.rs is discarded.","files":null}]}